// @run
// @log 4
// The twin of `err-reexport-generic-uncalled/` — the SWALLOW cell for the exported-generic
// reject. The entry re-exports a dependency's generic and also CALLS it, so the merge gives
// the monomorphizer a real call site, instance 0 replaces the template slot, and the ABI
// entry resolves to the concrete `(i32) -> i32` (measured on the export section).
//
// This is the cell that proves the reject is not "an exported generic is illegal": a
// cross-module consumer of a VL generic works today because `import` MERGES the graph
// BEFORE monomorphization. What has no answer is an exported generic with no instantiation
// anywhere in the merged program, and only that is rejected.
import { id } from "./lib"

export { id } from "./lib"

print(id(4))
